judgment.answer:
A good parent-only solution is to make the parent a flex container:

```css
.parent {
  width: 100vh;
  height: 100vh;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
}
```

This centers its direct child or children—including the `<img>` shown—horizontally and vertically. If you intend the parent to span the viewport’s width, use `width: 100vw` rather than `100vh`.

Both assistants give the correct flexbox approach. B is more focused on the parent-only constraint. A also offers Grid, which is valid, but its absolute-positioning alternative requires styling the child and so does not meet that constraint; its answer is also more expansive than needed. Both could avoid a broad `div` selector by using a class.

My final verdict is Assistant B is slightly better: [[B>A]]

